// rustc borrowck/borrowck-move-error-with-note.rs — upstream E0507 + E0509
// imported batch B167 @ da5114692c9ebe46b869488c5f34f92eb10b98c1 (rust 1.100.0)
// shelf: admit · root bck.E (was bck.B — RE-ROOTED, see below)
// ── BUCKET 3, BLESSED DIVERGENCE. 2026-09-07 (stage 5). RE-PORTED AS-IS FIRST, then
// classified: the port below IS upstream's construct, it IS admitted, and the
// admission is LICENSED — so the row is not a defect and leaves bc_admits.ledger.
// CLAUSE: docs/spec/expressions.md `intrinsic.drop.skip-moved-out-paths` —
//   "Drop of a value suppresses sub-values that were moved out". Rust's E0509
//   exists because `Drop::drop(&mut self)` needs the whole value intact; Logos
//   deliberately permits the move-out and skips the moved path at drop. The four
//   `*-with-dtor*` ports already in bc_admits_blocked.ledger sit under this same
//   clause, and they carry root `bck.E`, not `bck.B` — this row's `bck.B`
//   ("bookkeeping is ROOT-keyed and does not follow a projection") was assigned to a
//   program that has no projection in it. RE-ROOTED to `bck.E`.
// ⚠ RUN EVIDENCE, TAKEN AT THIS PORT'S OWN DOOR AND NOT INHERITED — 2026-09-07,
//   build b74842a0d915249f. `S`'s destructor was given a `putchar` body and `main`
//   printed a marker either side of `move_in_match()`. Output: `AB` — the user
//   `impl Drop for S` NEVER RAN. So the admission is blessed but the RUN is not
//   what the clause's own text requires (the value is still dropped; only the moved
//   leaf is skipped). That is NOT a new finding: this exact door is already
//   enumerated inside soundness_queue row `letstruct_destructure_skips_user_drop`
//   ("match door `match s { S { f: x, k: _ } => .. }` 100 THIS ROW"). The row for
//   the run-time half exists; this port adds no second one.
// Original path: tests/ui/borrowck/borrowck-move-error-with-note.rs
// upstream commit: 4b0c9d76ae7d387229caea55cfa73c280b08b8a7
// upstream construct: lines 29-36 (the `--b` suffix names upstream's SECOND
// annotated construct, `move_in_match`; its sibling `--a`, upstream's `blah2` at
// line 48, is already on the fail shelf) —
//     fn move_in_match() {
//         match (S {f: "foo".to_string(), g: "bar".to_string()}) {
//             //~^ ERROR cannot move out of type `S`, which implements the `Drop` trait
//             S { f: _s, g: _t } => {}
//         }
//     }
// Modifications:
//   · `"foo".to_string()` / `"bar".to_string()` -> `String::from("foo")` / `String::from("bar")`
//   · `Drop::drop` prints `self.f` upstream; here it clears it — a side effect on the
//     same field, with no formatting machinery
//   · assert -> exit code: `fn main() -> i32`
package borrowck_move_error_with_note_b;
use logos.mem.string;
struct S { f: String, g: String }
impl Drop for S { fn drop(self: &mut S) { self.f.clear(); } }
fn move_in_match() {
    match (S { f: String::from("foo"), g: String::from("bar") }) {
        S { f: _s, g: _t } => { }      // LEGAL LOGOS: intrinsic.drop.skip-moved-out-paths
    }
}
fn main() -> i32 {
    move_in_match();
    return 0i32;
}
